From cac7ff36f8cb01cb83ffcd71ccc9def261dc0808 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 30 Oct 2026 10:37:03 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(tui): route physical flushes through one seam so renderer
 tests stop timing the CI disk (#337)

* fix(tui): route physical flushes through one seam so renderer tests stop timing the CI disk [daveey]

Problem: PR328 run 41088210596 (job 124176079097, ec2-gwgen-3) failed ui-tui with 5000 ms
timeouts in busyQueueAdmission, canonicalStartupImage, pendingMigrationRenderer and
submissionDestinationRenderer. submissionDestinationRenderer then failed its second case with
queueRef.current []. The ui-tui tree is identical at merge-base b0df706, at the PR head and on main.
The earlier failure on the same runner (job 124096069004, receiptBusyRenderer) had the same shape.

Why 1: only the test files that do real fsyncs timed out. In the same job
CPU-heavy cursorDriftRegression (0 fsyncs) passed in 11.7 s. Every failing file performs 1-56 real
ext4 fsyncs per run (LD_PRELOAD counter).
Why 2: savePendingInput/removePendingInput (writeFileSync flush:true plus a directory fsync) and
stageImagePath (FileHandle.sync) flush to the physical disk on every queue mutation. In tests
that disk is the runner's, which is shared with the concurrent desktop/web checks. One fsync
waits on the journal commit behind their writeback (canonicalStartupImage timed out with 1 fsync).
Why 3: suites whose subject is queue/renderer logic ran the production flush, so their wall
time was set by other jobs' disk traffic, not by the code under test.
Root (happened): there was no seam between "write the journal" and "make it physically durable",
so tests could not keep the real journal without paying the device's flush latency.
Root (not caught): #326 reproduced with CPU stress and transport-latency seams only, never with
flush latency, and nothing stopped new direct fsync sites. Repro: an fsync-delay LD_PRELOAD
(400 ms per non-tmpfs fsync) fails these files on base every run; CPU-only stress passes 5/5.
Cascade: submissionDestinationRenderer tore down in a body-owned finally. A timed-out case's late
finally reset the next case's store and HERMES_HOME mid-assertion.

Countermeasures:
- src/lib/durableFlush.ts is the only flush site. pendingInputs writes temp, flushes the fd,
  renames, then flushes the directory, the same ordering as before. imageAttachments flushes
  through it.
- vitest.setup.ts replaces the flush with no-ops for every suite. Writes, renames and reads still
  hit the real journal. coldPendingRenderer's child process still runs the real syscalls.
- durableFlush.test.ts pins the ordering (complete temp record flushed before rename, directory
  flushed after rename and unlink, image bytes flushed before the path is returned) and checks
  that the real module issues the fsyncs. Moving either flush fails it.
- An eslint guard bans fsync/fdatasync imports, flush:true and .sync()/.datasync() outside the
  seam, so no new direct flush site can bring the disk back into renderer tests.
- submissionDestinationRenderer and busyQueueAdmission tear down in runner-owned afterEach.
  busyQueueAdmission's cleanup is idempotent, so a late finally becomes a no-op.
No assertion or timeout is changed.

* fix(tui): also ban member-call fsync outside the flush seam [daveey]
